     2	
     3	Built 2026-08-25. Temporary, gated access for the external party (https://the external party.ai / llm.the external party.ai) to
     4	benchmark the RTX Pro 6000 and the GB10. No production machine is directly reachable.
     5	
     6	## Architecture
     7	
     8	```
     9	the external party → the bench hostname  (CF Access, allowed-email policy, session 24h)
    10	     → cloudflared tunnel "the external party-bench-bastion" (4b1cfef2-549e-499e-8a07-fa6027db78fd)
    11	     → CT 204 the external party-bastion (node2, <lan-address> + <island-address> on island)
    12	        ├→ CT 205 the external party-bench-rtx  <island-address>:22  (isolated SDN vnet cirub0)
    13	        └→ GB10 the external party-bench container  <lan-address>   [NOT YET BUILT]
    14	```
    15	
    16	- **CF side** (account 3e3fc5cb…, zone <estate-domain>): tunnel `the external party-bench-bastion`,
    17	  CNAME `ssh-bench` → tunnel, Access app `the external party bench SSH` (493b20ef…), policy
    18	  `the external party-bench-allowed-emails` (43af5936…) — currently <email> only.
    19	- **CT 204 the external party-bastion** (1c/1G/8G, vmbr0 .204 + cirub0 <island-address>, onboot=1):
    20	  cloudflared connector; sshd key-only, user `the external party` (NO sudo); nftables drops all
    21	  private-range egress except <island-address>:22 and <lan-address>; tinyproxy
    22	  :8888 + dnsmasq :53 serve the island (only <island-address> allowed).
    23	- **CT 205 the external party-bench-rtx** (12c/32G/120G local-lvm, onboot=0, unprivileged):
    24	  on SDN vnet `cirub0` (zone `cirub`, node2-only, NO uplink — physical L2 island,
    25	  no NAT anywhere). GPU via /dev/nvidia* bind mounts + cgroup allows c 195/510
    26	  (same pattern as CT 201). NVIDIA user-space 610.43.02 via .run --no-kernel-modules
    27	  (matches node2 host driver EXACTLY — if node2 driver is upgraded, rerun the .run
    28	  in 205 with the new version). User `the external party` HAS passwordless sudo (his sandbox).
    29	  Internet only via bastion tinyproxy (http/https, set in /etc/profile.d/proxy.sh,
    30	  apt.conf.d/95proxy). Verified: HF reachable, Infisical/PBS NOT reachable.
    31	  Excluded from the hourly PBS job (exclude=114,205) — scratch box, 120G churn.
    32	- **GB10**: NOT built (permission classifier blocked remote execution on gb10; the owner
    33	  runs it). Staged script: `<path> external party-bench/gb10_setup.sh` — runs as `<login-user>`
    34	  (docker group, no sudo needed): `scp <path> external party-bench/gb10_setup.sh gb10:~ && ssh gb10 bash gb10_setup.sh`.
    35	  Design: docker-native only (no host iptables/systemd): bench container on an
    36	  `--internal` network (no outbound), socat relay container publishes .225:2222→bench:22,
    37	  squid proxy container (172.30.99.3:3128) allows internet but denies RFC1918.
    38	  All state under `/home/<login-user>/the external party-bench/` on gb10.
    39	
    40	## Access model — code-server behind CF Access (OTP), NO SSH key
    41	
    42	the external party reaches his sandboxes through a browser IDE (code-server), gated by Cloudflare
    43	Access with One-Time-PIN. No SSH key, no local `cloudflared`. Two factors: CF email-OTP
    44	+ the code-server password. Retired the SSH tunnel entirely (ssh-bench app/DNS/ingress
    45	deleted). The in-sandbox `sshd`/relays remain reachable ONLY from the bastion, as an
    46	admin fallback for us — not the external party-facing.
    47	
    48	- **URLs:** `https://bench-rtx.<estate-domain>` (RTX / CT 205), `https://bench-gb10.<estate-domain>`
    49	  (GB10 container). Access apps `the external party bench RTX/GB10 (code-server)`; policy allows
    50	  `jc@the external party.ai` + `<email>` via OTP.
    51	- **code-server:** CT 205 runs it as `the external party`, bound `<island-address>:8080` (systemd
    52	  `code-server.service`). GB10 runs container `the external party-code` (as `coder`, uid 1000) on the
    53	  internal net, reached via range-locked relay `the external party-code-relay` on `.225:8080`. Both use
    54	  the same argon2 `hashed-password` in config; the plaintext is NOT stored on the boxes
    55	  (hash only) — keep it in Infisical / your password manager.
    56	- **The bastion** proxies both: cloudflared HTTP ingress → `<island-address>:8080` /
    57	  `<lan-address>` (bastion nftables output opened for :8080 to both).
    58	- **Onboarding a new allowed user:** add their email to both Access policies
    59	  (`build/cf_add_email.py` pattern, or Zero Trust dash → Access → Applications), and give
    60	  them the two URLs + the code-server password. the external party submits jobs from the code-server
    61	  terminal with `the external party-submit` (see `queue/README.md`).
    62	
    63	### SSH (admin fallback only, not the external party-facing)
    64	`sshd` in CT 205 and the GB10 `the external party-bench` container is reachable only from the bastion
    65	(island / socat range-lock). We reach sandboxes directly via `pct exec 205` / `docker exec`,
    66	so this is a break-glass path; no external SSH ingress exists.
    67	
    68	## Benchmark windows (GPU exclusivity) — use the scripts
    69	
    70	Health-gated open/close (confirm VRAM actually freed on open; confirm prod is
    71	HEALTHY not just active on close — the estate's "active ≠ healthy" lesson):
    72	
    73	- **RTX:** `./window-rtx.sh open | close | status`. Stops/starts BOTH prod GPU
    74	  consumers on the card — `vllm.service` (gpt-oss-120b, :8000) AND
    75	  `vllm-embed.service` (:8100). `open` refuses to run during a vzdump. HAL chat
    76	  fails over down limp-router rungs during the window (degraded, not outage).
    77	- **GB10:** `./window-gb10.sh open | close | status`. Stops the 3 prod vLLM
    78	  containers, waits for unified memory to free (Grace-Blackwell reports VRAM
    79	  `used=[N/A]`, so readiness = containers exited + `free` available), raises the
    80	  bench mem cap to 115g of 121G usable (≈full box, ~6G host headroom); reverse to
    81	  20g on close. **Don't open while a tribunal panel
    82	  is running** — the Qwen3 seat (:8200) lives here.
    83	- Coordinate against hale-brain teacher/distillation runs on GB10.
    84	
    85	### Automated queue (booked blocks) — `queue/`
    86	For unattended runs, use the job queue instead of manual per-job windows: you open a
    87	booked block (`queue/the external party-queue --host rtx open 120`), the arbiter drains the external party's FIFO
    88	queue one job at a time on the full GPU, and prod comes back once at close. An
    89	independent dead-man on each GPU host restores prod if a window overruns or the arbiter
    90	dies. Built to the 2026-08-25 tribunal verdict with all six gates; see `queue/README.md`
    91	(operator usage) and `queue/CONTRACT.md` (design). the external party enqueues with `the external party-submit` in
    92	his sandbox; he never opens or extends a window.
    93	
    94	## Teardown (end of engagement) — use the script
    95	
    96	`./teardown.sh` (dry-run) → `./teardown.sh --commit`. Idempotent; removes CF
    97	Access app + policy + DNS + tunnel, both node2 CTs, the SDN vnet+zone, all GB10
    98	docker objects + state dir, and **removes only 205 from the backup exclude via
    99	compare-and-swap** (NOT a hardcoded `114` — that would clobber any co-tenant
   100	excluded mid-engagement). Then rotate anything of ours ever typed into the external party's
   101	env (should be nothing) and drop the memory note.
   102	
   103	## Security posture (updated 2026-08-25 after tribunal review)
   104	
   105	- **No inbound WAN exposure** anywhere (tunnel is outbound-only from CT 204).
   106	- **CF Access is the identity gate AND it is now load-bearing on-LAN too.**
   107	  Original build left both SSH entry points reachable directly from the flat
   108	  LAN (Access only gated WAN). Fixed: the bastion nftables no longer accepts
   109	  `:22` from the LAN (cloudflared reaches sshd on loopback only), and the GB10
   110	  relay uses `socat range=<lan-address>/32` so only the bastion may reach
   111	  `:2222`. Verified: a prod LAN host gets no banner from either; the bastion does.
   112	- Bastion user has no root; egress firewall + proxy policy live where he can't touch them.
   113	- CT 205 is on a no-uplink L2 island: LAN unreachable by construction (verified:
   114	  a prod host cannot reach <island-address>).
   115	- CT 205 is unprivileged; **GPU shared with CT 201 — isolation is fs/process-level,
   116	  not GPU-level.** A driver-crashing workload still hits prod vLLM; the window
   117	  scripts' health gates are the guard, not a hardware boundary.
   118	- GB10 bench container: `--internal` network, egress only via squid ACL, `HEALTHCHECK`
   119	  on sshd; setup script is idempotent with a health readiness gate (no blind sleep).
   120	
   121	### Known residual (non-blocking, needs one sudo on GB10)
   122	The GB10 `the external party-proxy` (squid) and `the external party-ssh-relay` (socat) are dual-homed on the
   123	default docker bridge (host-NAT-routable to the LAN). Containment against a
   124	*compromise of those helpers* rests on the squid ACL + socat range, not on
   125	routing. To close it, run once on GB10 as root:
   126	`iptables -I DOCKER-USER -s 172.17.0.0/16 -d <lan-address>/16 -j DROP` (plus
   127	10/8, 172.16/12) and persist. Deferred because `<login-user>` has no passwordless sudo.
     1	# the external party benchmark queue — contract & architecture
     2	
     3	Booked-block, operator-gated job queue built to the 2026-08-25 tribunal verdict
     4	(`../review/queue-tribunal-verdict.md`). Lets the external party submit benchmark jobs that run
     5	one-at-a-time on the full GPU during operator-opened windows, with production
     6	protected by the six required gates.
     7	
     8	## Roles & where they run
     9	- **Operator** (you) — opens/closes booked windows via `queuectl` on the management
    10	  host (this box). A cron you own can open windows on a schedule.
    11	- **Arbiter** — runs on the management host, one instance per GPU (`--host rtx|gb10`).
    12	  Single-writer of all queue/window/budget state. Drains the spool during an open
    13	  window. Reuses the health-gated `window-*.sh` (with fail-to-prod-UP rollback).
    14	- **Dead-man** — runs ON each GPU host, INDEPENDENT of the arbiter and the management
    15	  host. Restores prod if a window overruns its deadline or the arbiter's heartbeat
    16	  goes stale. This is the gate-1 safety net: prod-UP survives arbiter/host death.
    17	- **the external party** — writes job specs into his sandbox spool via `the external party-submit`; never controls
    18	  windows or preemption.
    19	
    20	## On-disk layout
    21	### Management host (arbiter state, single-writer)
    22	`~/.the external party-queue/<host>/`  (host = rtx|gb10)
    23	- `arbiter.lock`   — flock; only one arbiter per host runs.
    24	- `state.json`     — `{budget:{date, used_seconds}}`, updated atomically (tmp+rename).
    25	
    26	### GPU host (readable by the LOCAL dead-man, written by the arbiter over ssh)
    27	- node2: `/var/lib/the external party-queue/`   GB10: `/home/<login-user>/the external party-queue/`
    28	- `window.flag` — JSON `{open:bool, opened_at, expires_at, opened_by}`. Presence+expiry.
    29	- `heartbeat`   — epoch seconds; arbiter rewrites every 15s while alive.
    30	
    31	### Spool (in the external party's sandbox, host-visible on both GPUs)
    32	- GB10: host `/home/<login-user>/the external party-bench/scratch/queue` = container `/scratch/queue`.
    33	- RTX:  host `/var/lib/the external party-queue/spool` bind-mounted to CT 205 `/scratch/queue`.
    34	- `incoming/<id>.json`  — the external party's specs (he writes `*.json.tmp` then renames).
    35	- `running/<id>.json`   — arbiter claimed it (atomic rename from incoming).
    36	- `done/<id>/`          — `result.json` (status, exit, started/ended), `stdout`, `stderr`.
    37	
    38	## Job spec (`incoming/<id>.json`)
    39	```json
    40	{ "cmd": "python bench.py --model X",   // runs as user the external party INSIDE the sandbox
    41	  "est_seconds": 600,                    // the external party's estimate (advisory)
    42	  "max_seconds": 1800 }                  // hard per-job deadline; arbiter clamps to JOB_MAX
    43	```
    44	- `id` = the filename stem. `cmd` is untrusted; runs only in the external party's isolated,
    45	  cgroup-limited, ephemeral job context — never on the host.
    46	
    47	## Semantics (the six gates)
    48	1. **Fail-to-prod-UP dead-man** — independent per-host watchdog force-restores prod on
    49	   window-overrun or stale heartbeat, and on boot ensures prod-UP if no valid window.
    50	2. **Single-writer atomic state** — one arbiter (flock); every state write is tmp+rename;
    51	   queue claim is an atomic rename incoming→running; budget counter is monotonic per day.
    52	3. **Cgroup kill-tree per job** — RTX: `systemd-run --scope -p RuntimeMaxSec` (systemd
    53	   kills the whole cgroup at the deadline). GB10: ephemeral `docker run --rm` with
    54	   `--memory/--pids-limit/--cpus` + `timeout`, arbiter `docker kill` fallback.
    55	4. **Sandbox quotas** — arbiter refuses to claim when the spool exceeds MAX_SPOOL_BYTES
    56	   or MAX_SPOOL_FILES, and rejects a spec over MAX_SPEC_BYTES; per-job PID/CPU/mem caps
    57	   via the run context. (Hard fs-quota on GB10's dir needs root — documented, deferred.)
    58	5. **Restart retry + escalation** — window close retries prod restart; on failure it
    59	   leaves the window.flag CLOSED so the dead-man/next-open re-asserts prod-UP, and alerts.
    60	6. **Readiness probe** — before each job the arbiter verifies the sandbox exec responds
    61	   and (via window open) the GPU is actually free.
    62	
    63	## Booked-block model
    64	- `queuectl --host rtx open 120` → checks daily budget, runs `window-rtx.sh open`
    65	  (health-gated, rollback on fail), writes `window.flag{open, expires_at=now+120m}`.
    66	- The arbiter (already running) sees the open flag and drains the queue until the flag
    67	  closes or expires.
    68	- `queuectl --host rtx close` → `window-rtx.sh close` (retry), clears the flag.
    69	- Hard caps: `WINDOW_MAX_MIN` per window, `DAILY_BUDGET_MIN` total prod-downtime/day.
    70	  the external party cannot open, extend, or bypass these.
    71	
    72	## At-most-once guarantee
    73	Claim is `incoming→running` (atomic). On arbiter boot, any leftover `running/<id>.json`
    74	(a job interrupted by a crash) is moved to `done/<id>/` with `status:interrupted` and is
    75	**not** re-run. A job therefore runs zero or one times, never twice.
